  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure keeps the forecast largely unchanged. Dry weather\nand comfortable afternoon temperatures, especially with a \ngradual warming trend starting next week. The lowlands will be\nin the mid 60s to low 70s, with mid 70s by the end of the week.\nThe mountains will be in the 50s to mid 60s. These temperatures\nare around to slightly above the normal for the season. \n\nDew points will be a bit on the drier side with the lowlands\nstaying in the 40-50 range this week, while the mountains will\nbe in the 30-45 range. This will lead to dry RH values in the 30-50%\nrange each afternoon. Winds will be light or calm most of the\nweek though, so fire weather concerns will be low. \n\nAreas of dense fog is expected each morning through the week \nand patchy frost is also possible each morning through Monday,\nmostly across the mountain valleys, but the northern lowlands\ncould see it Sunday night/Monday morning with overnight lows in\nthe mid to upper 30s.\n\nA dry, backdoor cold front will approach the area Tuesday and\npark across our northern periphery. Rain chances may return by\nnext weekend as a cold front approaches from the west.",
